  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 PM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A warming trend will bring above average high \ntemperatures by mid to late week, along with dry conditions.\n\nLow pressure (~1006 mb), the remnants of this past weekend's \nNor'east, continues to weaken offshore of Long Island this \nafternoon. However, it maintains quite an impressive appearance in \nvisible satellite with a tightly- defined swirl and banding \nfeatures. A large area of high pressure is situated over the Midwest \nand this will gradually build eastward through midweek. This will \noccur as an upper-level ridge builds offshore of the Southeast \nCONUS. Temperatures will gradually warm through the week, with \nlegitimate hot temperatures for early October by Thursday and \nFriday. A few locations could flirt with the lower 90s on these \ndays, but especially on Friday. The sfc and upper-air synoptics \nsupport dry wx through most of the work week. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...An unsettled weather pattern with cooler and wetter \nconditions is possible by this weekend. \n\nHigh pressure shifts offshore Friday into Saturday. This will allow \nfor some sort of elongated frontal system and/or wave of low \npressure to approach the area during the upcoming weekend period, \nlikely from the S or SW. There remains considerable spread among the \nguidance on the track of these features, but some sort of cold front \nis likely to finally clear the area early next week. Ahead of this \nfront (and the aforementioned low/wave), precip chances increase, \nespecially by later Saturday through Sunday. There will also be a \nmodest increase in PWATs during this time. Increased clouds/precip \nand onshore flow favors cooler temps, but not abnormally so. Highs \nSaturday likely range through the 70s and 80s, with 70s Sunday.",
